#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055559} :activity/id #uuid "8a7c3e16-7b5a-517e-b02a-29c35f81f008" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050929 :topic/id #uuid "723d1f9c-b696-511c-b5e5-88c045fe7609" :topic/math-academy-id 50} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055559 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056050 :tutorial/content "Given two sets $A$ and $B,$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we say that $A$ is a **subset** of $B,$ and we write \n\n$$\nA \\subseteq B.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ {\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{blue}{3}} \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1,2,3\\}, \\qquad C=\\{1,2,{\\color{red}4}\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that *every* element of $A$ is also an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{1}}\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{3}}\\in B\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ is a subset of $B,$ and we write $A \\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $A$ and $B$ as shown below. Notice that $A$ is contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/91/911dbbe9a638810f3a245fdcd004e10aee4dbd49fd8a99741c1bdd5c48cc2319.png)\n\nOn the other hand, not every element of $C$ is an element of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n1 \\in B, \\qquad 2\\in B, \\qquad {\\color{red}4}\\notin B\n$$\n\nSince $4 \\notin B,$ we have that $C$ is *not* a subset of $B,$ and we write $C \\not\\subseteq B.$\n\nWe can visualize the sets $B$ and $C$ as follows. Notice that $C$ is *not* contained entirely within $B$ (here, $U$ is the universal set).\n\n![](images/45/454c28bacb8d5117bb11f76988b70344b4521d260adf2b690a11e7dfd2964385.png)\n\nAnother way of thinking about the statement $C \\not\\subseteq B$ is that $C$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $B.$ In this case, that element is $4.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2ef02f03-d232-5ab1-88fc-1f363535caa3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8240 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a4c7c6c6-5b36-53d1-a4de-4c76a981a433" :step/math-academy-id 152757 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056061 :tutorial/content "We note the following points regarding subsets:\n\n- Every set $A$ is a subset of itself:\n\n$$\nA\\subseteq A\n$$\n\nThis is because $A$ contains the same elements as $A,$ and therefore $A$ is a subset of $A.$\n\n- The empty set is a subset of *every* set. So, for all sets $A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\subseteq A.\n$$\n\nTo understand this, remember that $\\emptyset \\not\\subseteq A$ means that $\\emptyset$ contains *at least one* element that is not an element of $A.$ However, this cannot be true since $\\emptyset$ contains no elements! Thus, we must have $\\emptyset\\subseteq A.$\n\nIt's worth pointing out that the empty set is a subset of itself:\n\n$$\n\\emptyset \\subseteq \\emptyset\n$$\n\nFinally, note that a set $A$ is a **proper subset** of a set $B$ if $A \\subseteq B$ and $A \\neq B.$ In this case, we can write \n\n$$\nA \\subset B.\n$$\n\nTo explain this concretely, consider the following sets:\n\n$$\nA = \\{ 1,3 \\}, \\qquad B = \\{ 1, 2, 3 \\}, \\qquad C = \\{ 3,2,1 \\}\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a proper subset of $B,$ but $C$ is *not* a proper subset of $B{:}$\n\n- Since every element of $A$ is an element of $B,$ we have that $A\\subseteq B.$ Also, $A\\neq B.$ Therefore, $A$ is a *proper* subset of $B{:}$\n\n$$\nA\\subset B\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, $C\\subseteq B$ because every element of $C$ is an element of $B.$ However, $C$ is *not* a proper subset of $B$ because $C=B.$ Therefore, we write \n\n$$\nC \\not\\subset B.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1acb16ee-d59d-5050-9848-9cfdb6e2ee6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8316 :tutorial/title "Some Remarks"} :step/id #uuid "9cee0391-700f-550d-9b6e-139dae503e1e" :step/math-academy-id 152758 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054635 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054448 :question/id #uuid "70291b7a-ae46-5e8a-b24b-3ea343eb84f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-13195"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e5efaa-e624-5045-abdb-cdf8d32ad6cb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050373}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323902} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395713}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of a Given Set"} :step/id #uuid "b812cc2f-347b-5ed3-979d-e5bff1beb134" :step/math-academy-id 152759 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056053 :tutorial/content "We can systematically list all the subsets of a given set using a tree diagram.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find all the subsets of the set $A=\\{ 0,1 \\}.$\n\nWe list every possible combination of elements within the set using a tree diagram like the one below.\n\n![](images/dd/dd51f5e02c9f280b8478e11497602dd7ae886b84d1d5994d8af73dff9719d20e.png)\n\nWe start with the empty set as the root $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}}.$ \n\nNext, we consider the element $0\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset.\n\n- If we don't include $0$ in $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}},$ we are left with the empty set $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}}.$ Next, we consider the element $1\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset:\n\n- If we don't include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}},$ we are left with $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{\\}},$ we get $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{1\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $0$ in $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\{ \\}},$ we get the set $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}}.$ Next, we consider the element $1\\in A.$ We can either include it or not include it in a new subset:\n\n- If we don't include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}},$ we are left with $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0\\}}.$\n\n- If we include $1$ in $\\bbox[2pt,LightBlue]{\\{0\\}},$ we get $\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0,1\\}}.$\n\nFinally, the list of all subsets is shown in the last column:\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{1\\}},\\quad \\: \\bbox[2pt,Thistle]{\\{0,1\\}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "daebe910-6059-595b-a45a-fc890a9b1b53" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8244 :tutorial/title "Listing All the Subsets of a Set"} :step/id #uuid "547755c4-e17d-56bf-a674-91a455f0dbbf" :step/math-academy-id 152763 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055563}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054636 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054449 :question/id #uuid "0c50c147-4043-5aa0-b716-a9745206fdb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-13196"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbddaef-3828-5454-b892-26a3c6242ddb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351164}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding All Subsets of a Given Set"} :step/id #uuid "2e361262-f159-54e6-a942-659d5017ac7b" :step/math-academy-id 152764 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056062 :tutorial/content "Recall that the natural numbers, integers, rational numbers, and real and complex numbers can be arranged in the following hierarchical diagram.\n\n![](images/66/66260e4c239137cd30453d9335256fd7d84e93a197612d670c19d97e417bd2f0.png)\n\nIn other words, $\\mathbb{N}$ is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Z},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{Q},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{R},$ which is a (proper) subset of $\\mathbb{C}.$ We can write this symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z} \\subseteq \\mathbb{Q} \\subseteq \\mathbb{R} \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}\n$$\n\nSince none of these sets are equivalent, we can also write\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z} \\subset \\mathbb{Q} \\subset \\mathbb{R} \\subset \\mathbb{C}.\n$$\n\nSome restricted or extended special sets are subsets of others. For example:\n\n- $\\mathbb N$ is the set of natural numbers, and $\\mathbb N_0$ is the set of natural numbers *and* zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\mathbb N\\subset \\mathbb N_0.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Z$ is the set of integers, $\\mathbb Z^+$ is the set of positive integers (equivalent to $\\mathbb N$), and $\\mathbb Z^+_0$ is the set of positive integers and zero (equivalent to $\\mathbb N_0$). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z^+\\subset  \\mathbb Z_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb Z.\n$$\n\n- $\\mathbb Q^+$ is the set of positive rational numbers, $\\mathbb R^+$ is the set of positive real numbers, and $\\mathbb R$ is the set of real numbers. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Q^+\\subset  \\mathbb R^+ \\subset \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nHowever, note that, for example, $\\mathbb Q \\not\\subset  \\mathbb R^+,$ since $0\\in\\mathbb Q$ yet $0\\notin \\mathbb R^+.$\n\nFrom these basic inclusions, we can now deduce some subset relationships for other special numerical sets. For example, recall that [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] represents the set of all non-negative integers. \n\n* Obviously, [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] is a proper subset of [math]\\mathbb{Z}[/math] since all the elements of [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] are integers but there are some integers, say, [math]-1,[/math] that does not belong to [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+.[/math] So, [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb{Z}.[/math]\n\n* On the other hand, [math]\\mathbb{N}[/math] is a proper subset of [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] since all natural number lie in [math]\\mathbb{Z}_0^+[/math] but [math]0 \\notin \\mathbb{N}.[/math] So, [math]\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_0^+.[/math]\n\nTherefore, we have [math]\\mathbb{N} \\subset \\mathbb{Z}_0^+ \\subset \\mathbb{Z}.[/math]" :tutorial/id #uuid "c336106d-52a5-5d56-a98f-c64cd26eef4b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8317 :tutorial/title "Subsets of Special Sets"} :step/id #uuid "5386697a-6e4f-5619-83d7-53f1064c31f2" :step/math-academy-id 152767 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054637 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054450 :question/id #uuid "f2840f6f-da8b-519d-a191-f19d37fa7957" :question/math-academy-id "e-13197"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049893}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399137}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets"} :step/id #uuid "053e7255-8298-5305-9c62-d263e5b3b5ba" :step/math-academy-id 152768 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055566 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056079 :tutorial/content "The notation \n\n$$\nA \\supseteq B\n$$\n\nreads \"$A$ is a **superset** of $B$\", and is equivalent to $B \\subseteq A.$ This is similar to how we can write either $x \\leq y$ or $y \\geq x$. The two notations mean the same thing. \n\nSimilarly, $A \\supset B$ reads \"$A$ is a **proper superset** of $B$,\" and is equivalent to $B \\subset A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d87be2be-dac5-52e2-bcbd-7c0ad0d17032" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9065 :tutorial/title "Superset Notation"} :step/id #uuid "d545e0c5-338a-5d32-8901-ef36ab148f6e" :step/math-academy-id 152771 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054638 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054451 :question/id #uuid "e8db159d-7a1b-52ab-be96-6b1fd3eabb29" :question/math-academy-id "e-13198"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351179}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets"} :step/id #uuid "7b56c42d-9c31-515e-ae69-cd2b8675b68c" :step/math-academy-id 152772 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055602}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056081 :tutorial/content "You might wonder why we need to define a universal set. Can't we just let the universal set be the set of everything?\n\nThe answer is no. If we don't specify a well-defined universal set, we encounter a nonsensical situation called **Russell's paradox.**\n\nFirst, suppose there exists a set $\\Omega$ that contains absolutely everything, including every mathematical object, every thought, every book, every person, animal, cell, and star in the universe, past, present, or future. \n\nRussell's paradox originates from the following idea:\n\n> *Define $S$ as the set that contains all sets that are not elements of themselves.*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> S = \\{A\\in \\Omega\\,:\\, A\\notin A\\}\n> $$\n\nMost sets you can think of are elements of $S.$ For example, the set $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}$ is in $S$ because $\\{ 1,2,3 \\}\\notin \\{ 1,2,3 \\}.$ \n\nIt takes a bit of thought to find sets that *aren't* members of $S.$ Such sets do exist, although typical examples are somewhat abstract: \n\n- The set containing all possible sets.\n\n- The set containing all possible thoughts.\n\n- The set $X,$ given by\n\n$$\nX = \\Big\\{\\underbrace{\\big\\{ \\left\\{\\{\\ldots \\}\\right\\}\\big\\}}_{X}\\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nHowever, what about $S$ itself? Is $S$ an element of $S?$ There are only two possibilities:\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction because we cannot have $S\\in S$ *and* $S\\notin S.$\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we must have that $S\\in S.$ But this leads to the same contradiction as before!\n\nThe only way to resolve this is to conclude that $S\\notin \\Omega,$ but this is also a contradiction since $\\Omega$ contains absolutely everything!  So, there is no consistent answer to the question of whether $S$ itself is an element of $S.$ This is Russell's paradox, and when it was discovered, it shook the foundations of mathematics to its very core.\n\nThe issue, unknown to mathematicians until later, is that the object [math]S[/math] is not a set. If it were, Russell's paradox would not occur since we should always be able to determine whether an object is a member of a set or not.\n\nLater, it was realized that if a set is defined using a predicate, we should ensure that all sets involved are subsets of some larger set $U$ (i.e., a universal set) where we can always determine which elements are members of $U$ and which are not, at least in principle.\n\nTo see how this resolves Russell's paradox, suppose $U$ is a universal set. Let's refine our definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{A\\subseteq U\\,:\\,A\\notin A\\}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume $S$ is a set, so $S\\subseteq U$ for some well-defined $U.$ Once again, we'll ask the question of whether $S\\in S{:}$\n\n- If $S\\in S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\notin S.$ But this is a contradiction.\n\n- If $S\\notin S,$ then by the definition of $S,$ we have that $S\\in S.$ But this is also a contradiction.\n\nThus, to resolve the paradox, we're left with no choice but to conclude that $S\\not\\subseteq U.$ This indicates that something is fundamentally wrong with our definition of $S,$ and no such set can exist because if it were a set, it would belong to a universal set. \n\n* If [math]S\\notin S,[/math] then by the definition of [math]S[/math] and applying De Morgan's law for conjunctions, we have\n\\begin{align*}\nS\\notin S \\Leftrightarrow \\lnot (S\\subseteq U\\land  S\\notin S)  \\equiv (S\\not\\subseteq U) \\lor (S\\in S).\n\\end{align*}" :tutorial/id #uuid "0e2d16c6-3382-5ba2-8d4b-949482eb4f07" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9388 :tutorial/title "Russell's Paradox: Why We Need a Universal Set"} :step/id #uuid "7280da1b-4410-550f-9a00-d46456526bfc" :step/math-academy-id 164820}] :activity/title "Subsets" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054915}]}
